---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import headshot from '../assets/headshot.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About · ${site.name}`}
  description="Joshua Ford: Director of NetSuite Administration, amateur radio operator (KJ5IRQ), and the person who puts APIs on both."
>
  <article class="about">
    <header>
      <p class="label">About</p>
      <h1>The operator</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="about-grid">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I'm Joshua Ford. By day I am Director of NetSuite Administration for a multi-company
          rural ISP. At home I am {site.callsign}. The through-line is the same: I run systems,
          and I build the missing interface when the one I was given is dumb.
        </p>

        <h2>The arc</h2>
        <p>
          I started in warehouse operations, learned the systems that ran the floor, and kept
          following them upstream. National warehouse manager, then inventory and fixed assets,
          then the ERP itself. I helped select NetSuite, stood it up from nothing, and I am still
          the sole administrator.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is not a title with a team under it doing the work. I write the SuiteScript, run
          the integrations, train the people who had never used an ERP, and take the ugly findings
          to finance leadership myself.
        </p>

        <h2>What I actually do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            NetSuite OneWorld across multiple subsidiaries: WMS, Smart Count, SCM, FAM,
            intercompany, consolidated reporting
          </li>
          <li>SuiteScript 1.0 and 2.x, SuiteQL, SuiteFlow, saved searches, custom records</li>
          <li>Procure-to-pay, order-to-cash, record-to-report, period close</li>
          <li>AP automation, FP&A, TMS, REST and SOAP integrations, sandbox-before-production</li>
          <li>
            An MCP server on the live ERP so language models can query SuiteQL without getting the
            keys
          </li>
          <li>Roles, least privilege, training, and governance that survive real users</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Same habit, off the clock</h2>
        <p>
          My radio node has a REST API and an MCP server because SSH and DTMF were a dumb control
          plane. My homelab exists so experiments break at home. I present on AI for ham operators
          on <a href="/talks/">Absolute Tech</a>. The pattern does not change with the domain.
        </p>

        <h2>What I work with</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>NetSuite OneWorld, SuiteScript, SuiteQL, SuiteFlow, WMS</li>
          <li>Python, FastAPI, JavaScript, SuiteQL, PostgreSQL, Docker, Linux, Git</li>
          <li>Model Context Protocol, local-first AI, Claude / OpenAI / Ollama</li>
          <li>AllStarLink, EchoLink, amateur radio infrastructure</li>
          <li>Proxmox and home automation</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Reaching me</h2>
        <p>
          Email is best: <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. The resume version of
          me is on <a href={site.linkedin} rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>. The code lives on
          <a href={site.github} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>. The callsign checks out on
          <a href={site.qrz} rel="noopener">QRZ</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Image
        src={headshot}
        alt="Joshua Ford"
        width={400}
        widths={[400, 800]}
        sizes="(max-width: 800px) 60vw, 280px"
        class="about-photo"
      />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about header {
    padding-block: var(--space-8) var(--space-6);
  }

  .about-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--measure)) auto;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    align-items: start;
    padding-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .about-photo {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    width: 280px;
    height: auto;
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--space-6);
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .about-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .about-photo {
      width: 200px;
      position: static;
      order: -1;
    }
  }
</style>
